WEBVTT

1
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:22.689
<i>Previously on</i>
Star Trek: Strange New Worlds...

2
00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:24.190
<i>Commander Pelia.</i>

3
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:25.900
this is Lieutenant Scott.
He needs your help.

4
00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:28.903
- I know Scotty.
- Professor.

5
00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.946
<i>Is it not tradition</i>

6
00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:33.033
<i>to make
a grand romantic gesture.</i>

7
00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:36.077
<i>or have I again mistaken
how human relationships work?</i>

8
00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:37.620
<i>I thought you and Christine</i>

9
00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:39.288
had decided
to give each other some space.

10
00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:41.124
<i>Indeed.</i>

11
00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:43.626
- Spock.
- Christine.

12
00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:46.504
This is... Dr. Korby.

13
00:00:47.422 --> 00:00:49.090
My date.

14
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.728
Captain.

15
00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:04.314
we're picking up
that space radiation again.

16
00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:07.692
Whatever this is

17
00:01:07.817 --> 00:01:10.820
does not appear to be native
to the Blootar Nebula.

18
00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:13.782
This is space. Lieutenant.

19
00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:17.285
There's no such thing as...
native.

20
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.497
We're all children...
of the cosmos.

21
00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:24.918
Cap. in the past hour. we have
had 14 more crew members report

22
00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:27.087
to medbay
on account of melancholia.

23
00:01:27.212 --> 00:01:29.464
I have seen this
only once before...

24
00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:31.508
in the war.

25
00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:33.468
Then we'll need a leg up.

26
00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.553
But Captain. we're outside
of regulation space.

27
00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:39.557
Don't lecture me
on jurisdiction. Lieutenant!

28
00:01:39.682 --> 00:01:44.604
I wrote the book
on space... jurisdiction.

29
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.274
And I am known for my...

30
00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:50.401
diction.

31
00:01:51.528 --> 00:01:53.488
Now...

32
00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:57.909
...get me... the Agonyans.

33
00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:02.455
The Agonyans are extremely
dangerous space creatures.

34
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.249
You don't think I know that?!

35
00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:06.751
Make the call. Lieutenant.

36
00:02:06.876 --> 00:02:10.547
for the sake
of the Blootar Nebula and space.

37
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.593
Captain. I have the Agonyans
on the space-phone.

38
00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:17.512
On viewer-screen.

39
00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:20.765
Greetings
from the USS <i>Adventure.</i>

40
00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.518
I am...
the captain of this vessel.

41
00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:26.980
representing the whole of the
Alliance of Outer Space Planets.

42
00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:31.484
<i>I am Zipnop
of the Triathic Agonyan Empire.</i>

43
00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:37.240
All worship to us. small.
pathetic. human food-piles.

44
00:02:37.365 --> 00:02:40.118
We scanned an unusual level
of radiation

45
00:02:40.243 --> 00:02:41.911
in the Blootar Nebula.

46
00:02:42.078 --> 00:02:46.791
Even though this is outside of
your empire's... jurisdiction.

47
00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:48.751
<i>That is a lie!</i>

48
00:02:48.877 --> 00:02:51.337
Spread by pathetic
but delicious humans.

49
00:02:51.462 --> 00:02:53.464
And yet... Doctor.

50
00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:56.301
have we not received
multiple cases of melancholia

51
00:02:56.426 --> 00:02:58.678
- aboard this ship?
- We have. Cap.

52
00:02:58.803 --> 00:03:00.762
And doesn't
melancholia only occur

53
00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:03.892
when humans are exposed
to Agonyan radiation waves?

54
00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.312
- True.
- Check and... mate.

55
00:03:07.437 --> 00:03:10.857
<i>Argh! You have figured out
our plan.</i>

56
00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:14.485
We may need your brain cells
to power our radiation.

57
00:03:14.611 --> 00:03:16.696
but you will never stop us!

58
00:03:16.821 --> 00:03:18.907
We can. and we will
because we must.

59
00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:22.202
It is our duty...
as space explorers.

60
00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.412
Not if we destroy you first.

61
00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:25.872
I'd like to see you try.

62
00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:27.665
But Captain. what if they can?

63
00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.793
<i>You humans are rich
with brain cells.</i>

64
00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:32.420
<i>If you give them to us.</i>

65
00:03:32.587 --> 00:03:34.881
<i>we will choose</i> not
<i>to destroy you.</i>

66
00:03:35.006 --> 00:03:36.841
<i>You have</i>

67
00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:40.303
one minute to comply
with our wishes.

68
00:03:41.596 --> 00:03:45.558
The Agonyans have put us
in an impossible situation.

69
00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:48.353
There is... only one solution.

70
00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:51.606
We can't give them
our brain cells.

71
00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.943
We need it...
for many science reasons.

72
00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:58.029
Captain. the Agonyans

73
00:03:58.154 --> 00:04:01.491
are firing a series
of nuclear lasers at us.

74
00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:03.576
Shields up! Brace for impact!

75
00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:13.795
Fire photonos beams at them!

76
00:04:16.798 --> 00:04:18.883
They're gone.

77
00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:22.095
And so is...

78
00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:23.846
- our brain cells.
-No!

79
00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:25.348
What do we do?

80
00:04:28.685 --> 00:04:32.021
First... we find the Agonyans...

81
00:04:32.897 --> 00:04:35.400
...retrieve our... brain cells.

82
00:04:35.525 --> 00:04:37.277
and then...

83
00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:38.778
we get back our joy.

84
00:04:43.866 --> 00:04:46.452
<i>the last frontier.</i>

85
00:04:46.577 --> 00:04:49.163
<i>This is the tale of
the USS</i> Adventure

86
00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.582
<i>on its 84-month mission</i>

87
00:04:51.708 --> 00:04:54.711
<i>as it treks across the furthest
reaches of the universe.</i>

88
00:04:54.877 --> 00:04:57.212
<i>striving to find unknown life.</i>

89
00:04:57.213 --> 00:05:01.634
<i>unexplained phenomena.
and new space destinations.</i>

90
00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:04.178
<i>To go forward. to go beyond.</i>

91
00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:07.432
<i>to go further and bolder
than anyone ever before.</i>

92
00:05:46.179 --> 00:05:48.930
<i>Security Officer's Log.</i>

93
00:05:48.931 --> 00:05:51.933
Enterprise <i>is about to pass
through the Kitolian Belt.</i>

94
00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:54.020
<i>which centers on a neutron star.</i>

95
00:05:54.145 --> 00:05:57.439
<i>But while</i> Enterprise <i>studies
the neutron star.</i>

96
00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.277
<i>I've been assigned a separate
but equally important task.</i>

97
00:06:01.444 --> 00:06:05.615
<i>Call it a dual mission.</i>

98
00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:10.370
You want me to...
play a game. sir?

99
00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.622
Starfleet believes that
they have figured out a solution

100
00:06:12.747 --> 00:06:14.582
to long-term.
faster-than-light travel.

101
00:06:14.749 --> 00:06:16.918
Starships will soon be able
to go for more

102
00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:18.836
than five years
without returning to Federation.

103
00:06:18.961 --> 00:06:22.965
So. games and entertainment as
distraction and stress release.

104
00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:25.385
Now the device we're going
to be testing is called

105
00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.261
the "Re-creation Room"?

106
00:06:27.387 --> 00:06:28.763
Holodeck. for short.

107
00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:30.264
- Sounds better.
-Yeah.

108
00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.433
Is this based
on battle simulators?

109
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.519
'Cause I-I've beaten
every one of those.

110
00:06:34.644 --> 00:06:36.354
Why do you think we picked you?

111
00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:38.564
But those simulators are usually
on starbases.

112
00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:41.442
They take up huge amounts
of energy and processing power.

113
00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.652
Can our ship actually handle it?

114
00:06:43.653 --> 00:06:45.613
That's what we're here
to figure out.

115
00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:47.240
Our orders are clear.

116
00:06:47.365 --> 00:06:48.908
While <i>Enterprise</i> studies
the collapsing neutron star.

117
00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:51.077
you're going
to test the holodeck.

118
00:06:51.202 --> 00:06:53.162
Put it through its paces
under a real mission.

119
00:06:53.287 --> 00:06:55.331
Show it could work under
genuine. rigorous circumstances.

120
00:06:55.498 --> 00:07:00.002
How rigorous do you expect a
game on the holodeck to be. sir?

121
00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:03.005
Well. it's a test. Lieutenant.

122
00:07:03.172 --> 00:07:05.716
The kind of thing I'd do all the
time when I was a test pilot.

123
00:07:05.842 --> 00:07:07.301
Which means.

124
00:07:07.468 --> 00:07:10.012
you push it as hard as you can.

125
00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:12.974
You told me
you needed a challenge.

126
00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:15.184
You're welcome.

127
00:07:27.238 --> 00:07:29.365
Am I interrupting?

128
00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.284
In lieu of my normal
morning calisthenics.

129
00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:33.161
I have elected
to continue dance training.

130
00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:34.703
I have nearly
completed my routine.

131
00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:36.539
Oh. don't stop on my account.

132
00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:41.794
Computer. begin playlist:
Spock 19.

133
00:07:46.048 --> 00:07:48.468
Tango.

134
00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:50.470
Ambitious.

135
00:07:51.262 --> 00:07:53.097
I just swung by to ask
about the field emitters

136
00:07:53.222 --> 00:07:56.184
for the holodeck that
you've been fixing for Scotty?

137
00:07:57.894 --> 00:07:59.562
Eyes up. Lieutenant.

138
00:08:00.146 --> 00:08:02.732
You told me the eyes are
the anchor of the tango.

139
00:08:02.857 --> 00:08:05.193
Are they not?

140
00:08:11.908 --> 00:08:13.951
My diagnostics
are nearly complete.

141
00:08:14.076 --> 00:08:16.662
I have realigned
the field emitters.

142
00:08:16.787 --> 00:08:18.498
The holodeck should be
operational as soon

143
00:08:18.623 --> 00:08:20.791
as you and Mr. Scott finish
designing a program for you.

144
00:08:20.917 --> 00:08:23.961
- Thank you.
- Has the monitoring pin
been much of a burden?

145
00:08:24.086 --> 00:08:25.922
I actually forgot
I was wearing it.

146
00:08:26.047 --> 00:08:28.090
That is a good thing.

147
00:08:28.257 --> 00:08:29.967
It is best if it captures you
in a natural state.

148
00:08:30.092 --> 00:08:31.719
so the holodeck can construct
the most realistic environment

149
00:08:31.886 --> 00:08:33.763
for your specifications.

150
00:08:33.888 --> 00:08:37.183
Speaking of. have you chosen
a subject for your simulation?

151
00:08:37.308 --> 00:08:38.935
Not yet.

152
00:08:40.478 --> 00:08:42.355
I am not well-versed
in human entertainment.

153
00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:45.441
but violence and relaxation seem
to be popular.

154
00:08:45.608 --> 00:08:47.777
Along with...

155
00:08:49.654 --> 00:08:51.822
...romantic narratives.

156
00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:59.205
Amelia.

157
00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:01.749
Amelia Moon?
What the hell is that?

158
00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:04.377
Detective stories
from Earth in the 1960s.

159
00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:06.295
The-the captain of the ship
that rescued me

160
00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.756
from the Gorn--
he-he gave me a copy.

161
00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:12.009
and I became obsessed.

162
00:09:12.134 --> 00:09:14.971
Amelia was an outsider.
but she always found herself

163
00:09:15.096 --> 00:09:17.807
in the middle of a murder.
or a kidnapping.

164
00:09:17.932 --> 00:09:20.351
or a heist gone wrong.

165
00:09:20.476 --> 00:09:23.521
And she always got justice.

166
00:09:23.646 --> 00:09:25.565
no matter what.

167
00:09:26.983 --> 00:09:28.359
Let's just say...

168
00:09:28.484 --> 00:09:29.651
she had an effect on me.

169
00:09:29.652 --> 00:09:32.113
There are 112 of them
in the database.

170
00:09:32.238 --> 00:09:33.573
That's plenty of material
to form the simulation.

171
00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:36.200
Although. the computer might

172
00:09:36.325 --> 00:09:39.494
have trouble keeping
the simulation running as it is.

173
00:09:39.495 --> 00:09:40.955
Uh. Pelia's on shore leave.

174
00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:42.748
so. you-you want
to call in Spock again?

175
00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:44.834
It's nothing I can't handle.

176
00:09:45.001 --> 00:09:46.711
I-I can balance
the processing needs

177
00:09:46.836 --> 00:09:48.713
from the rest of the ship.

178
00:09:48.838 --> 00:09:50.631
Although.
there is one wee wrinkle.

179
00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:53.092
Uh. the holographic characters

180
00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:56.137
need to be incredibly detailed
to pass muster.

181
00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:59.181
The only bio-signatures
we have at that resolution

182
00:09:59.307 --> 00:10:01.225
are from the transporter
pattern buffer.

183
00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:02.893
We'll use their likenesses

184
00:10:03.019 --> 00:10:05.396
to create holographic
interactive avatars.

185
00:10:05.521 --> 00:10:08.316
Ready when you are. Lieutenant.

186
00:10:10.318 --> 00:10:14.113
using the Amelia Moon
novels as reference

187
00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:17.992
and factoring in my skills
as an investigator.

188
00:10:18.117 --> 00:10:22.997
create a new mystery that I will
find challenging to solve.

189
00:10:31.464 --> 00:10:32.632
Spock. I...

190
00:10:32.798 --> 00:10:34.342
I thought you'd finished
running diagnostics.

191
00:10:34.508 --> 00:10:36.510
I wanted to ensure the
computer system is functioning

192
00:10:36.636 --> 00:10:37.803
at normal parameters.

193
00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:39.220
I appreciate your concern.

194
00:10:39.221 --> 00:10:41.641
But. uh. don't tell Scotty.

195
00:10:41.766 --> 00:10:43.434
He's been working very hard
on this.

196
00:10:43.559 --> 00:10:46.228
Indeed. He is sensitive.

197
00:10:46.354 --> 00:10:49.398
You may run and evaluate
the program on your command.

198
00:10:53.903 --> 00:10:55.237
...run program.

199
00:11:00.993 --> 00:11:03.704
I can practically smell
the ocean and the cigarettes.

200
00:11:03.871 --> 00:11:06.165
It would be unwise to smoke
in the confines of the holodeck.

201
00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:07.333
Or at all.

202
00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.335
No. I just meant
it's-it's very. um...

203
00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.587
Lifelike. Indeed.

204
00:11:11.754 --> 00:11:15.800
I have spent so many hours
reading about Amelia. I just...

205
00:11:15.925 --> 00:11:18.719
I just can't believe
I actually get to <i>be</i> her.

206
00:11:18.844 --> 00:11:20.513
Well. as my ancestor.

207
00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:23.057
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle would
write. "The game is afoot."

208
00:11:23.224 --> 00:11:25.309
Thank you. Mr. Spock.

209
00:11:25.434 --> 00:11:28.270
but this is
a single-player situation.

210
00:11:28.396 --> 00:11:29.980
I am available.

211
00:11:30.106 --> 00:11:32.149
should you decide
you need my assistance.

212
00:11:32.274 --> 00:11:34.360
Good luck. Lieutenant.

213
00:11:37.279 --> 00:11:39.073
Amelia Moon!

214
00:11:39.198 --> 00:11:41.116
- Thank goodness you're here.
- Uhura.

215
00:11:41.117 --> 00:11:42.702
Joni Gloss. We met briefly

216
00:11:42.827 --> 00:11:44.829
when you handled
that Fox fiasco last November.

217
00:11:44.954 --> 00:11:46.287
Saved my client's career.

218
00:11:46.288 --> 00:11:48.582
and kept him out of San Quentin.
Now. follow me.

219
00:11:48.708 --> 00:11:49.958
I'm the one who found the body.

220
00:11:49.959 --> 00:11:51.419
Of course. I did.

221
00:11:51.544 --> 00:11:52.586
I'm always the first to arrive.

222
00:11:52.712 --> 00:11:53.754
And I have a rule in this town.

223
00:11:53.879 --> 00:11:55.214
If you're on time. you're late.

224
00:11:55.339 --> 00:11:57.591
And if you're late. you're dead.

225
00:11:57.717 --> 00:12:00.177
In this case. literally.

226
00:12:00.302 --> 00:12:01.762
Not by my hand. of course.

227
00:12:01.929 --> 00:12:03.139
You'll put that in your record?

228
00:12:04.515 --> 00:12:08.144
Only. you weren't the first
guest to arrive. were you?

229
00:12:08.269 --> 00:12:09.395
One of them killed Tony.

230
00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:10.980
I just don't know who.

231
00:12:11.147 --> 00:12:12.690
That's why I called you.

232
00:12:12.815 --> 00:12:14.442
I need you to solve this

233
00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:15.693
before the cops show up.

234
00:12:15.818 --> 00:12:16.861
They will arrest every one of us

235
00:12:16.986 --> 00:12:18.362
just to sell the story to

236
00:12:18.487 --> 00:12:20.072
the gossip rags. and then. poof.
before you know it.

237
00:12:20.239 --> 00:12:22.116
half my clients. out of a job.

238
00:12:22.283 --> 00:12:23.492
So. protect your clients.

239
00:12:23.617 --> 00:12:25.745
Except the murderer. obviously.

240
00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:27.246
I'm going to drop them.

241
00:12:27.371 --> 00:12:29.999
Where's the body?

242
00:12:33.002 --> 00:12:34.502
In there.

243
00:12:34.503 --> 00:12:36.714
Staying honest.

244
00:12:36.839 --> 00:12:38.424
I could barely look at him.

245
00:12:38.549 --> 00:12:40.676
I'll stall the others
in the sitting room

246
00:12:40.843 --> 00:12:44.638
while you go do
what it is you... do.

247
00:12:44.764 --> 00:12:46.140
Got a light?

248
00:12:47.391 --> 00:12:48.642
Fantastic.

249
00:12:49.643 --> 00:12:51.645
<i>Hollywood. California.</i>

250
00:12:51.812 --> 00:12:53.856
<i>Mansions. movie stars.</i>

251
00:12:53.981 --> 00:12:55.775
<i>and murder.</i>

252
00:12:57.318 --> 00:12:58.986
Strangulation.

253
00:12:59.111 --> 00:13:00.780
But with what?

254
00:13:00.905 --> 00:13:01.989
<i>At 9:00 p.m. tonight.</i>

255
00:13:02.156 --> 00:13:03.491
<i>Tony Hart.</i>

256
00:13:03.616 --> 00:13:05.659
<i>head of Lomond Picture Studios</i>

257
00:13:05.785 --> 00:13:07.661
<i>was strangled in his own home.</i>

258
00:13:07.828 --> 00:13:09.497
<i>But with what?</i>

259
00:13:09.663 --> 00:13:10.872
<i>And by whom?</i>

260
00:13:10.873 --> 00:13:13.000
<i>And don't forget about
the "why."</i>

261
00:13:13.125 --> 00:13:15.085
<i>Find your motive.</i>

262
00:13:15.211 --> 00:13:16.378
<i>and you've got your killer.</i>

263
00:13:16.504 --> 00:13:17.797
<i>Usually. a motive hides</i>

264
00:13:17.922 --> 00:13:19.715
<i>quiet secrets hidden in shadows</i>

265
00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:21.258
<i>and behind closed doors.</i>

266
00:13:21.383 --> 00:13:23.677
<i>but I could see
this case had it all.</i>

267
00:13:23.844 --> 00:13:26.013
<i>Passion. desperation.</i>

268
00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:27.848
<i>jealousy. rage.</i>

269
00:13:28.015 --> 00:13:29.724
<i>And as for the suspects.</i>

270
00:13:29.725 --> 00:13:32.311
<i>you may recognize
some of the guests from</i>

271
00:13:32.478 --> 00:13:35.481
<i>Lomond's television space
adventure hour.</i>

272
00:13:35.606 --> 00:13:38.400
The Last Frontier.

273
00:13:38.526 --> 00:13:40.986
<i>including the show's creator
TK Bellows</i>

274
00:13:41.111 --> 00:13:44.323
<i>and former actress
turned producer Sunny Lupino.</i>

275
00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:46.033
<i>The show's stars--
Adelaide Shaw.</i>

276
00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:49.244
<i>Maxwell Saint and Lee Woods--
are also here.</i>

277
00:13:49.245 --> 00:13:52.623
<i>along with Shaw's main squeeze.
Anthony MacBeau.</i>

278
00:13:52.748 --> 00:13:55.835
<i>And you already met
power agent Joni Gloss.</i>

279
00:13:56.585 --> 00:13:59.254
Your host. Tony Hart. is dead.

280
00:13:59.255 --> 00:14:01.674
and one of you murdered him.

281
00:14:05.594 --> 00:14:07.304
Hold your horses.

282
00:14:07.429 --> 00:14:09.849
Why would one of us
want to murder Hart?

283
00:14:09.974 --> 00:14:11.851
Because your show was
about to be canceled.

284
00:14:11.976 --> 00:14:15.062
Canceled?
But we're a hit.

285
00:14:17.523 --> 00:14:20.234
Oh. Ratings.

286
00:14:21.151 --> 00:14:23.112
Now. here's how this
is going to go.

287
00:14:23.237 --> 00:14:25.573
No one is gonna
walk out of that door

288
00:14:25.698 --> 00:14:27.741
or make any phone calls.

289
00:14:27.908 --> 00:14:30.035
Even you. William Morris.

290
00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:31.452
We're all gonna get real cozy

291
00:14:31.453 --> 00:14:33.289
until we've worked this all out.

292
00:14:33.414 --> 00:14:34.957
This can't be happening. To me.

293
00:14:35.124 --> 00:14:36.834
What we know:

294
00:14:36.959 --> 00:14:39.295
At 9:00 p.m. tonight.
someone strangled Tony Hart

295
00:14:39.461 --> 00:14:41.171
with an unknown weapon
in his bedroom.

296
00:14:41.297 --> 00:14:43.132
We know the killer
is in this room.

297
00:14:43.299 --> 00:14:45.926
and we know the likely motive
is the cancelation

298
00:14:46.051 --> 00:14:48.429
of <i>The Last Frontier.</i>

299
00:14:50.055 --> 00:14:51.807
And the key to this puzzle...

300
00:14:52.933 --> 00:14:55.686
...is as clear as glass.

301
00:15:00.649 --> 00:15:01.901
What?

302
00:15:02.026 --> 00:15:05.529
Wait. it wasn't...
it wasn't me.

303
00:15:05.654 --> 00:15:08.157
Everyone knows your drink. pal.

304
00:15:08.324 --> 00:15:10.034
I believe you.

305
00:15:10.159 --> 00:15:12.202
Besides. you favor
a frostier shade of pink.

306
00:15:12.328 --> 00:15:15.289
But this shade is classic.

307
00:15:15.456 --> 00:15:16.916
Max Factor's Ruby Red.

308
00:15:17.041 --> 00:15:18.918
released in 1955. and splashed

309
00:15:19.084 --> 00:15:20.336
all over fashion magazines

310
00:15:20.502 --> 00:15:24.173
on the iconic face of
model and pop star

311
00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:26.258
Sunny Lupino.

312
00:15:26.383 --> 00:15:28.802
What can I say.
I'm a trendsetter.

313
00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:31.138
But that doesn't
make me a killer.

314
00:15:32.973 --> 00:15:34.934
That doesn't.

315
00:15:35.059 --> 00:15:37.645
But this does.

316
00:15:39.063 --> 00:15:42.023
<i>The Last Frontier</i> was your
first project as a producer.

317
00:15:42.024 --> 00:15:43.609
You fought to get it on the air.

318
00:15:43.734 --> 00:15:45.361
leveraged your
relationships with the studio

319
00:15:45.527 --> 00:15:46.946
and your ex-husband.

320
00:15:47.071 --> 00:15:48.489
Bet your whole career on it.

321
00:15:48.656 --> 00:15:51.200
Tony and I were divorced.
but we were still

322
00:15:51.367 --> 00:15:53.202
very much invested
in each other's success.

323
00:15:53.369 --> 00:15:55.955
You were a little more invested
than most. weren't you?

324
00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:59.707
It's expensive to bring space
into American living rooms. so

325
00:15:59.708 --> 00:16:02.544
you took a personal loan from...

326
00:16:03.712 --> 00:16:05.923
...the mob.
But if the show was canceled.

327
00:16:06.048 --> 00:16:09.385
you'd never be able
to pay it back. so...

328
00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:12.846
...you killed Tony
to keep it on the air.

329
00:16:12.972 --> 00:16:16.100
The only thing I know about
that man is that he's a fan.

330
00:16:16.225 --> 00:16:18.185
He wanted an autograph.

331
00:16:18.352 --> 00:16:19.937
Why the telelens. then?

332
00:16:20.062 --> 00:16:23.107
I was having dinner
at Perino's with Johnny.

333
00:16:23.232 --> 00:16:26.485
Carson. He was trying to
talk me into a project at NBC.

334
00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:28.654
Tony was keeping tabs on me

335
00:16:28.779 --> 00:16:30.531
to make sure
I wasn't being unfaithful.

336
00:16:30.656 --> 00:16:31.907
To the studio.

337
00:16:32.032 --> 00:16:33.741
But you're right.

338
00:16:33.742 --> 00:16:35.076
I did invest my own money.

339
00:16:35.077 --> 00:16:36.954
I just didn't
take it from the mob.

340
00:16:37.079 --> 00:16:38.831
I sold the house.

341
00:16:38.956 --> 00:16:41.000
Mine and Tony's first house.

342
00:16:41.125 --> 00:16:42.917
Oh. but you love that house.

343
00:16:42.918 --> 00:16:44.085
Until I convinced Alfred to

344
00:16:44.086 --> 00:16:45.421
put me in <i>Crows.</i> I was just

345
00:16:45.546 --> 00:16:47.506
another pair of lips.

346
00:16:48.257 --> 00:16:49.883
But that Oscar opened doors.

347
00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:53.261
And what did I use it for.
my own star vehicles?

348
00:16:53.262 --> 00:16:55.931
No. When I heard TK's pitch

349
00:16:56.056 --> 00:16:59.100
for <i>The Last Frontier.</i> I knew

350
00:16:59.101 --> 00:17:00.352
it was special.

351
00:17:00.519 --> 00:17:01.979
And you'd do anything
to save it.

352
00:17:02.104 --> 00:17:03.439
You're damn right. I would.

353
00:17:03.605 --> 00:17:05.190
I even came to Tony with a plan.

354
00:17:05.315 --> 00:17:07.192
- But he refused.
- He agreed.

355
00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:08.527
We were going over the details

356
00:17:08.694 --> 00:17:09.777
when Maxwell Saint
interrupted us.

357
00:17:09.778 --> 00:17:11.238
If you really want to know

358
00:17:11.363 --> 00:17:12.406
what happened to Tony.

359
00:17:12.573 --> 00:17:13.907
talk to him.

360
00:17:26.211 --> 00:17:27.546
Oh. my God.

361
00:17:30.299 --> 00:17:32.760
Lee? Is she dead?

362
00:17:32.926 --> 00:17:35.262
You know I'm an actor.
not a doctor. right?

363
00:17:35.387 --> 00:17:36.764
But. yeah. I mean. look at her.

364
00:17:36.889 --> 00:17:38.682
She's obviously dead.

365
00:17:47.024 --> 00:17:48.776
Poison.

366
00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:55.282
-Scotty. you busy?
<i>-A bit busy. yeah.</i>

367
00:17:55.449 --> 00:17:56.909
<i>Your simulation is pulling</i>

368
00:17:57.034 --> 00:17:58.702
<i>a wee bit more power
than expected. Why?</i>

369
00:17:58.869 --> 00:18:00.870
Base a holodeck
on a battle simulator

370
00:18:00.871 --> 00:18:02.581
- and it's going to act like one.
<i>- What does that mean?</i>

371
00:18:02.706 --> 00:18:05.292
Well. it's figured me out.
learned my moves.

372
00:18:05.417 --> 00:18:08.587
- Just like an enemy opponent.
<i>- Well. how can I change that?</i>

373
00:18:08.712 --> 00:18:10.005
No. I need a fresh eye.

374
00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:11.673
someone who won't
see things like I do.

375
00:18:11.799 --> 00:18:14.468
In book number 57.
<i>Murder at the Paper Moon Inn.</i>

376
00:18:14.593 --> 00:18:16.095
Amelia Moon needed a partner.

377
00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:17.721
<i>Well. I'm afraid it can't be me</i>

378
00:18:17.846 --> 00:18:20.140
<i>if you want the Enterprise
to continue running.</i>

379
00:18:20.265 --> 00:18:22.142
Oh. no. that's okay.

380
00:18:23.393 --> 00:18:25.854
I think I might have the perfect
dance partner already.

381
00:18:27.397 --> 00:18:30.109
Spock. thank you for coming.

382
00:18:30.234 --> 00:18:32.945
I'm here to assist
in any way I can.

383
00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:34.113
Though. may I ask.

384
00:18:34.238 --> 00:18:35.489
if the holodeck
has defeated you.

385
00:18:35.656 --> 00:18:36.906
why not just end the mission?

386
00:18:36.907 --> 00:18:39.742
Look. Pike gave me
very clear orders:

387
00:18:39.743 --> 00:18:42.538
Test the device
under rigorous circumstances.

388
00:18:42.704 --> 00:18:43.913
Also. it didn't defeat me.
I leveled up

389
00:18:43.914 --> 00:18:45.624
and then it
increased the difficulty.

390
00:18:45.749 --> 00:18:47.209
Besides.

391
00:18:47.376 --> 00:18:49.586
Amelia Moon doesn't give up
until she gets her man.

392
00:18:49.711 --> 00:18:51.171
and neither do I.

393
00:18:51.296 --> 00:18:52.881
I have briefed myself
on the case.

394
00:18:53.006 --> 00:18:55.175
What was Detective Moon's
partner like?

395
00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:57.010
- Proficient. I assume?
- He was a bumbling idiot.

396
00:18:57.177 --> 00:18:59.054
- That does not seem useful.
- No.

397
00:18:59.221 --> 00:19:00.848
You will be much more so.

398
00:19:01.682 --> 00:19:02.766
Who's this guy?

399
00:19:02.891 --> 00:19:04.184
Detective Spock.

400
00:19:04.309 --> 00:19:05.602
He works with me.

401
00:19:05.769 --> 00:19:07.229
I didn't know this was
a pajama party.

402
00:19:07.396 --> 00:19:08.564
Hang on.

403
00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:11.358
Did Central Casting send you?

404
00:19:11.483 --> 00:19:12.651
What. with those ears?

405
00:19:12.776 --> 00:19:14.361
Detective Spock
doesn't watch television.

406
00:19:14.486 --> 00:19:15.821
So he's a communist?

407
00:19:15.946 --> 00:19:18.866
More likely
one of those cinema snobs.

408
00:19:19.032 --> 00:19:21.326
MacBEAU:
What does it matter?

409
00:19:21.451 --> 00:19:24.413
The holo-Kirk seems
particularly agitated.

410
00:19:24.538 --> 00:19:27.749
He should. he's number one
on my suspect list.

411
00:19:27.916 --> 00:19:30.126
Thanks for coming so fast.

412
00:19:30.127 --> 00:19:32.462
These fluctuations... here.

413
00:19:32.588 --> 00:19:34.131
They're becoming more frequent.

414
00:19:34.256 --> 00:19:36.175
We are six hours
from a collapsing star.

415
00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:38.051
Minor surges are to be expected.

416
00:19:38.218 --> 00:19:39.595
Everyone's pulling
an all-nighter.

417
00:19:39.761 --> 00:19:41.555
I even recommended Pike
reinstate Ortegas.

418
00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:42.639
And you trust her?

419
00:19:42.806 --> 00:19:44.933
- I do.
-Auxiliary power

420
00:19:45.058 --> 00:19:46.894
also seems to be having issues.

421
00:19:48.562 --> 00:19:50.814
This seems beyond standard.

422
00:19:53.066 --> 00:19:55.569
Don't worry. I've got
everything under control.

423
00:19:55.694 --> 00:19:57.905
Wow. that is a sensory overload.

424
00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:00.324
Oh. all normal.
all under control.

425
00:20:00.449 --> 00:20:02.492
The omnidirectional
holo-diodes produce

426
00:20:02.659 --> 00:20:05.787
a tremendous number of variables
to keep an eye on.

427
00:20:05.954 --> 00:20:08.457
- Ah.
- Do you need
a second set of eyes?

428
00:20:08.624 --> 00:20:09.917
No. I've got it.

429
00:20:10.042 --> 00:20:12.377
In my experience.
sometimes a second set of eyes

430
00:20:12.502 --> 00:20:14.922
can be... detrimental.
I-I can handle it.

431
00:20:15.047 --> 00:20:18.050
Okay. but I do need to sign off
on your engineering logs

432
00:20:18.175 --> 00:20:19.760
before returning to work
on the neutron star.

433
00:20:19.885 --> 00:20:22.930
Fingers crossed you improve
upon the haphazard example

434
00:20:23.055 --> 00:20:24.389
set by your...

435
00:20:24.514 --> 00:20:25.766
supervisor.

436
00:20:25.891 --> 00:20:27.476
Yes. I'm familiar with. uh.

437
00:20:27.601 --> 00:20:29.352
Pelia's infamous logs.

438
00:20:29.353 --> 00:20:32.022
An eagle could go blind
from reading them.

439
00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:34.690
Oh...

440
00:20:34.691 --> 00:20:36.026
- Broken already?
- Oh. no. no.

441
00:20:36.193 --> 00:20:37.611
Just a wee bug.
to be expected with

442
00:20:37.778 --> 00:20:39.279
beta phase testing.

443
00:20:39.404 --> 00:20:41.864
You know. a simulator this big
will automatically pull

444
00:20:41.865 --> 00:20:43.867
processing power to compensate.
but. you know.

445
00:20:44.034 --> 00:20:45.327
it's not a problem.

446
00:20:45.494 --> 00:20:49.831
I'll just. um.
divert a little juice. Yeah.

447
00:20:49.957 --> 00:20:51.625
La'An may be running this test.

448
00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:53.502
but. uh.

449
00:20:53.627 --> 00:20:55.504
you're the failsafe.

450
00:20:58.757 --> 00:21:00.383
Understood. Loud and clear.

451
00:21:00.384 --> 00:21:01.802
But. you know. I've got it.

452
00:21:06.223 --> 00:21:09.935
Mr. Saint. did you kill
Tony Hart and Sunny Lupino?

453
00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:11.436
Nope.

454
00:21:11.561 --> 00:21:12.562
Did you?

455
00:21:12.729 --> 00:21:13.897
Sonny said you and Tony

456
00:21:14.064 --> 00:21:15.899
were fighting before he died.

457
00:21:16.066 --> 00:21:18.193
A tiff. A row.

458
00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:19.861
A tangle. maybe.

459
00:21:20.028 --> 00:21:22.030
It's not even worth discussing.

460
00:21:24.366 --> 00:21:25.492
It was jealousy.

461
00:21:25.659 --> 00:21:26.910
Jealousy?

462
00:21:28.829 --> 00:21:30.579
Your cuff.

463
00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:32.874
There was an identical one
at the crime scene.

464
00:21:33.834 --> 00:21:37.838
A little trinket
from Hart's secretary.

465
00:21:38.714 --> 00:21:39.548
She likes me.

466
00:21:39.715 --> 00:21:41.591
You were having an affair.

467
00:21:42.551 --> 00:21:43.844
You caught me.

468
00:21:44.011 --> 00:21:45.512
And Mr. Hart had an issue
with this?

469
00:21:45.679 --> 00:21:47.014
Well. only because
he was having an affair

470
00:21:47.139 --> 00:21:48.849
- with the same secretary.
-Hmm.

471
00:21:49.016 --> 00:21:50.183
sounds complicated.

472
00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:51.435
No. not really. It was casual.

473
00:21:51.601 --> 00:21:53.854
You work closely with people.

474
00:21:53.979 --> 00:21:55.814
and things happen.

475
00:22:03.322 --> 00:22:05.741
You two must know how that goes.

476
00:22:05.907 --> 00:22:07.326
What? We--

477
00:22:08.118 --> 00:22:10.370
No. this-this is about you.

478
00:22:10.537 --> 00:22:12.039
not... anyone else.

479
00:22:12.205 --> 00:22:13.915
Look. I'm an actor.

480
00:22:14.082 --> 00:22:15.250
I like drama.

481
00:22:15.417 --> 00:22:17.461
but it's certainly
nothing to kill for.

482
00:22:17.627 --> 00:22:19.546
And besides. I had already
convinced Hart that

483
00:22:19.713 --> 00:22:21.423
it was over
between me and the secretary.

484
00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:23.133
And how did you do that?

485
00:22:24.051 --> 00:22:25.427
Me and Maxwell?

486
00:22:25.552 --> 00:22:27.763
Absolutely 100% never.

487
00:22:27.888 --> 00:22:29.389
You seem awfully heated
about that.

488
00:22:29.514 --> 00:22:32.351
He's a chauvinistic egomaniac.

489
00:22:33.226 --> 00:22:35.479
I can barely stand to be
on set with the guy.

490
00:22:35.604 --> 00:22:37.397
Why would I go to bed with him?

491
00:22:37.522 --> 00:22:39.733
Were you aware that
the show was being cancelled?

492
00:22:40.609 --> 00:22:41.693
I heard whispers.

493
00:22:41.818 --> 00:22:43.278
How did you feel about it?

494
00:22:43.445 --> 00:22:44.988
Well.

495
00:22:45.155 --> 00:22:46.573
my lines and my skirts have been

496
00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:48.784
getting shorter and shorter
with every episode.

497
00:22:48.909 --> 00:22:50.577
and the only person

498
00:22:50.744 --> 00:22:53.288
who ever fought for
my character was Sunny.

499
00:22:53.455 --> 00:22:56.291
And now she's dead. so...

500
00:22:57.084 --> 00:22:59.961
Did you know that I was just 18
when I played

501
00:23:00.087 --> 00:23:01.755
Hedda Gabler on the West End?

502
00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:05.842
I'm good at what I do.
Detectives.

503
00:23:05.967 --> 00:23:08.929
But all anyone seems
to care about is your love life.

504
00:23:09.096 --> 00:23:10.305
Must be frustrating.

505
00:23:10.472 --> 00:23:12.432
Yeah. it is.

506
00:23:12.557 --> 00:23:14.643
But is it wrong to want love

507
00:23:14.768 --> 00:23:17.229
and to be taken seriously
in my career?

508
00:23:20.023 --> 00:23:21.775
So. why do you think there are
persistent rumors

509
00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:23.819
about your affair
with Mr. Saint?

510
00:23:29.199 --> 00:23:30.700
How did you feel
about Miss Lupino?

511
00:23:30.867 --> 00:23:34.037
You mean. did I have a reason
to kill her?

512
00:23:34.162 --> 00:23:36.039
No.

513
00:23:36.206 --> 00:23:37.541
I admired her.

514
00:23:37.666 --> 00:23:41.878
She couldn't settle
for being a work of art.

515
00:23:42.045 --> 00:23:44.423
She wanted to make art.

516
00:23:44.548 --> 00:23:46.049
Art means a lot to you.

517
00:23:46.216 --> 00:23:47.676
It's everything.

518
00:23:47.801 --> 00:23:50.720
If I can give a gift
to the world.

519
00:23:50.846 --> 00:23:52.347
I want to help people unleash

520
00:23:52.472 --> 00:23:54.391
the artistic beast
inside of them.

521
00:23:54.558 --> 00:23:58.019
How does one
"unleash the artistic beast"?

522
00:23:58.145 --> 00:24:00.272
I've been doing
an artists salon.

523
00:24:00.397 --> 00:24:02.399
Lee probably told you about it.

524
00:24:02.566 --> 00:24:04.860
What goes on
at an artists salon?

525
00:24:05.026 --> 00:24:06.570
Mac plays guitar.

526
00:24:06.695 --> 00:24:08.780
Others sing. paint.

527
00:24:08.905 --> 00:24:10.824
- And you?
- Lee writes.

528
00:24:10.949 --> 00:24:12.868
She's been writing
a screenplay. actually.

529
00:24:12.993 --> 00:24:14.828
- A movie.
<i>-A screenplay?</i>

530
00:24:14.953 --> 00:24:16.163
Why?

531
00:24:16.288 --> 00:24:17.539
Mac told you that?

532
00:24:17.664 --> 00:24:20.959
A western set in the Dakotas.

533
00:24:21.084 --> 00:24:22.669
There was a script
in the victim's room.

534
00:24:22.794 --> 00:24:24.087
about the Dakotas.

535
00:24:25.464 --> 00:24:27.132
Who else knew about this?

536
00:24:32.429 --> 00:24:34.264
Guilty conscience
got you running?

537
00:24:34.389 --> 00:24:36.600
More like a weak stomach.

538
00:24:36.766 --> 00:24:39.436
Must be hard to watch something
you've poured so much into

539
00:24:39.603 --> 00:24:41.605
just disappear.

540
00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:44.024
It's not my
favorite feeling. no.

541
00:24:44.149 --> 00:24:46.610
But the ratings were too low
to warrant a season two?

542
00:24:46.776 --> 00:24:50.030
Our fanbase is small.
but it's quite passionate.

543
00:24:50.155 --> 00:24:51.573
Were you threatened

544
00:24:51.698 --> 00:24:53.325
by the Western motion picture
Miss Woods wrote?

545
00:24:53.450 --> 00:24:56.620
Lee wrote another Western?
Good for her.

546
00:24:56.745 --> 00:24:58.747
She'll never tire
of that dying genre.

547
00:24:58.914 --> 00:25:00.832
So. you knew about the script?

548
00:25:00.957 --> 00:25:04.002
You weren't jealous?

549
00:25:04.127 --> 00:25:06.379
What about the cancellation?
Did you know about that. too?

550
00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:10.592
Is that why you ripped up
your contract? Out of anger?

551
00:25:12.344 --> 00:25:15.096
Do you know what they say about
a show after its first season?

552
00:25:15.222 --> 00:25:17.557
No. What do they say?

553
00:25:17.682 --> 00:25:19.476
Practically writes itself.

554
00:25:20.977 --> 00:25:22.562
Hart ripped up that contract.
not me.

555
00:25:22.687 --> 00:25:24.147
He was actually
doing me a favor.

556
00:25:24.272 --> 00:25:26.149
You wanted to leave the show?

557
00:25:26.316 --> 00:25:28.735
Warners has the rights
to a book I've been chasing.

558
00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.195
They're even gonna
let me direct.

559
00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:33.281
Yeah. I-I-I love our show.

560
00:25:33.406 --> 00:25:35.492
but movies...

561
00:25:35.617 --> 00:25:38.286
That's how you change
hearts and minds. kids.

562
00:25:38.453 --> 00:25:40.372
So. you're happy the show
was being canceled?

563
00:25:40.497 --> 00:25:42.874
Oh. Detective.

564
00:25:42.999 --> 00:25:44.334
I'm a writer.

565
00:25:44.459 --> 00:25:46.461
I'm never happy about anything.

566
00:25:47.212 --> 00:25:48.838
But I didn't kill anyone.

567
00:25:56.346 --> 00:25:57.931
They all worked together.

568
00:25:58.056 --> 00:25:59.349
yet allowed their emotions
to jeopardize

569
00:25:59.474 --> 00:26:01.351
the very work
that they all shared.

570
00:26:06.022 --> 00:26:07.148
What is that?

571
00:26:07.274 --> 00:26:09.359
Lee Woods's script.

572
00:26:09.484 --> 00:26:10.986
I knew something
was off about it.

573
00:26:11.111 --> 00:26:13.363
Look. no title page.

574
00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:14.739
But why?

575
00:26:15.865 --> 00:26:17.701
Perhaps to hide the connection
to the writer.

576
00:26:17.826 --> 00:26:20.537
No. no. no. not from Bellows.
He didn't even care.

577
00:26:21.538 --> 00:26:22.330
No.

578
00:26:23.873 --> 00:26:26.459
What if Lee's not the one
who removed it?

579
00:26:26.585 --> 00:26:27.877
La'An...

580
00:26:31.464 --> 00:26:32.882
The chandelier
appears to have been

581
00:26:33.008 --> 00:26:34.342
purposefully cut to harm us.

582
00:26:34.467 --> 00:26:36.386
To stop us

583
00:26:36.511 --> 00:26:38.054
because we're close.

584
00:26:38.221 --> 00:26:40.765
The screenplay
doesn't have a title page

585
00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:42.642
because it was stolen.

586
00:26:42.767 --> 00:26:45.270
Hart stole Lee's script and was
going to credit someone else.

587
00:26:45.395 --> 00:26:48.231
probably a man. because that
happened all the time back then.

588
00:26:49.399 --> 00:26:51.234
So she killed him for it.

589
00:26:58.366 --> 00:26:59.993
There is a more pressing matter.

590
00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:02.537
Blood?

591
00:27:02.662 --> 00:27:04.080
Well. I-I thought the holodeck

592
00:27:04.205 --> 00:27:05.624
had safety protocols.

593
00:27:05.749 --> 00:27:07.667
Perhaps they are malfunctioning.

594
00:27:09.002 --> 00:27:11.087
We should get out of here.
not risk anything else.

595
00:27:11.254 --> 00:27:13.798
Computer. end program.

596
00:27:16.217 --> 00:27:19.554
Mr. Scott. please end program.

597
00:27:22.849 --> 00:27:24.726
Computer. arch.

598
00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:28.772
So...

599
00:27:28.938 --> 00:27:30.940
we can't shut it off.

600
00:27:31.066 --> 00:27:33.360
the program
could actually hurt us.

601
00:27:33.485 --> 00:27:35.028
and we can't get out.

602
00:27:35.153 --> 00:27:36.655
Indeed.

603
00:27:45.372 --> 00:27:46.956
Uh...

604
00:27:48.083 --> 00:27:50.085
Lieutenant Scott.
are you looking for me?

605
00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:53.672
Say I've got this hypothetical
communications problem.

606
00:27:53.797 --> 00:27:56.383
could you advise me
on this problem that. again.

607
00:27:56.508 --> 00:27:57.884
you know.
it's not a real problem.

608
00:27:58.009 --> 00:28:00.553
in the traditional sense
of the word "problem."

609
00:28:00.679 --> 00:28:02.389
- It's...
- I think I followed that.

610
00:28:02.555 --> 00:28:04.516
In the highly unlikely event

611
00:28:04.641 --> 00:28:06.935
that I were to have lost
all contact with La'An

612
00:28:07.102 --> 00:28:10.063
inside the holodeck.
what would you advise?

613
00:28:10.188 --> 00:28:12.732
Uh... Well. if comms failed.

614
00:28:12.857 --> 00:28:15.193
I'd bypass local subprocessors
and patch directly

615
00:28:15.318 --> 00:28:16.528
into the optical data network.

616
00:28:16.653 --> 00:28:18.947
Yeah. Imagine
in this hypothetical.

617
00:28:19.072 --> 00:28:21.783
like. I've already done that.
multiple times.

618
00:28:21.908 --> 00:28:25.620
Hypothetically.
I'd shut the simulation down.

619
00:28:25.787 --> 00:28:27.580
- Uh...
- Which. I'm assuming.

620
00:28:27.706 --> 00:28:29.749
you've already.
hypothetically. done.

621
00:28:29.916 --> 00:28:32.961
Scotty. are you telling me La'An
is trapped in the holodeck?

622
00:28:34.421 --> 00:28:36.673
Have you tried
knocking on the door?

623
00:28:36.798 --> 00:28:38.174
Or simply blowing it off?

624
00:28:38.299 --> 00:28:40.385
The holodeck is a complex
balance of forcefields.

625
00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:42.887
tractor beams.
and precision-guided diodes.

626
00:28:43.012 --> 00:28:44.556
all hardwired in.

627
00:28:44.681 --> 00:28:46.725
So. blowing it up would crash
our entire computer system.

628
00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:48.601
Permanently.
Per my calculations.

629
00:28:48.768 --> 00:28:50.353
the only way
to unlock the program

630
00:28:50.478 --> 00:28:51.604
is for the lieutenant
to complete it.

631
00:28:51.771 --> 00:28:52.814
but she's unaware of this.

632
00:28:52.939 --> 00:28:54.274
What does completing it entail?

633
00:28:54.399 --> 00:28:56.651
Solving a murder mystery
set in 1969 Hollywood.

634
00:28:58.945 --> 00:29:00.488
I'm on it.

635
00:29:00.613 --> 00:29:02.115
- Thank you. Ensign.
- Yeah.

636
00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:04.659
Eh. oh. you-you know
that wasn't all hypothetical?

637
00:29:04.784 --> 00:29:06.327
Yeah. I caught on
almost immediately.

638
00:29:06.453 --> 00:29:07.620
Okay.

639
00:29:10.123 --> 00:29:12.625
Unfortunately. I was unable
to access the panel

640
00:29:12.751 --> 00:29:13.877
while in the simulation.

641
00:29:14.002 --> 00:29:15.003
I still can't reach Scotty
over comms

642
00:29:15.128 --> 00:29:16.045
or anyone on the ship.

643
00:29:16.171 --> 00:29:17.255
So. we have a computer problem

644
00:29:17.380 --> 00:29:18.506
and a communications problem.

645
00:29:18.631 --> 00:29:20.216
We also have a murderer problem.

646
00:29:20.341 --> 00:29:22.718
If the killer comes after us.
we could actually die in here.

647
00:29:22.719 --> 00:29:26.639
So maybe the only way to survive
is to catch the murderer.

648
00:29:26.765 --> 00:29:28.850
Which means...

649
00:29:28.975 --> 00:29:30.435
we're going to go
solve this mystery.

650
00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:31.811
I-I just...

651
00:29:31.936 --> 00:29:33.980
can't believe
you wanted off the show.

652
00:29:34.147 --> 00:29:36.065
You also wanted off the show.

653
00:29:36.191 --> 00:29:38.234
You know what I think?
I think you hate me.

654
00:29:38.359 --> 00:29:40.820
and you hate the fact
that I gave you the best job

655
00:29:40.945 --> 00:29:42.697
that you're ever gonna have.

656
00:29:42.864 --> 00:29:44.240
Are you threatening me?

657
00:29:44.365 --> 00:29:45.784
Did you all hear that?

658
00:29:45.909 --> 00:29:47.118
TK threatened me!

659
00:29:47.243 --> 00:29:48.244
MacBEAU:
He tells no lies.

660
00:29:48.369 --> 00:29:49.621
What would you know?

661
00:29:49.746 --> 00:29:51.706
You're only here
as Adelaide's escape hatch.

662
00:29:51.831 --> 00:29:52.874
What is that
supposed to mean?

663
00:29:53.041 --> 00:29:55.710
Audiences don't like you.

664
00:29:55.835 --> 00:29:59.214
At least I can deliver
a line realistically.

665
00:29:59.339 --> 00:30:00.256
It's called acting.

666
00:30:00.381 --> 00:30:01.841
- Oh.
- And you know

667
00:30:01.966 --> 00:30:03.343
what's not realistic?
A lady first officer.

668
00:30:03.468 --> 00:30:05.970
Face it.
You knew your days were limited.

669
00:30:06.095 --> 00:30:07.847
That's why you agreed to marry
Mick Bowie over here.

670
00:30:07.972 --> 00:30:09.974
so you could disappear on tour
as his groupie

671
00:30:10.099 --> 00:30:11.935
and nobody would notice
that your career was dead!

672
00:30:12.060 --> 00:30:15.730
Everyone shut up. now!

673
00:30:16.773 --> 00:30:17.857
Right.

674
00:30:18.024 --> 00:30:20.568
Where is Lee Woods?

675
00:30:20.693 --> 00:30:24.781
And who is this guy?

676
00:30:28.409 --> 00:30:30.161
She's dead.

677
00:30:31.037 --> 00:30:32.205
Joni Gloss.

678
00:30:32.372 --> 00:30:33.915
It's not what it looks like.

679
00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:35.500
I was just looking for a light.

680
00:30:35.625 --> 00:30:36.793
She was dead when I found her.

681
00:30:36.918 --> 00:30:38.711
You had quite a bit
of cash to lose

682
00:30:38.837 --> 00:30:40.463
from the cancelation of
<i>The Last Frontier.</i> didn't you?

683
00:30:40.588 --> 00:30:42.423
I had three clients
on that show. You do the math.

684
00:30:42.549 --> 00:30:44.717
But I didn't kill Tony Hart.

685
00:30:44.884 --> 00:30:46.261
though I wish I had.

686
00:30:46.427 --> 00:30:47.762
And why is that?

687
00:30:48.888 --> 00:30:50.306
Because canceling
<i>The Last Frontier</i>

688
00:30:50.431 --> 00:30:52.100
is the stupidest thing
Tony's ever done.

689
00:30:52.225 --> 00:30:55.562
Do you realize how rare
a show like this is?

690
00:30:55.728 --> 00:30:58.439
TK wanted to give audiences
a digestible reflection

691
00:30:58.565 --> 00:31:01.860
of their own world
through the lens of fantasy.

692
00:31:01.985 --> 00:31:04.320
Social commentary
with rubber masks

693
00:31:04.445 --> 00:31:07.740
and buried metaphors.
You know. science fiction.

694
00:31:07.866 --> 00:31:10.243
And the studio did not agree
with this vision?

695
00:31:10.410 --> 00:31:12.912
They were more...

696
00:31:13.079 --> 00:31:14.330
cautious.

697
00:31:14.455 --> 00:31:16.916
Didn't want to "make waves."
The cowards.

698
00:31:17.083 --> 00:31:19.752
TK wanted <i>Frontier</i>
to take our rotten world.

699
00:31:19.878 --> 00:31:22.255
with its warts and injustices.

700
00:31:22.422 --> 00:31:24.299
and give audiences
something better.

701
00:31:24.424 --> 00:31:27.051
And all of that
with weekly space adventures?

702
00:31:27.176 --> 00:31:29.846
You don't find
the idea inspiring?

703
00:31:29.971 --> 00:31:32.640
You don't think some kid out
there is going to see this show

704
00:31:32.807 --> 00:31:34.976
and spend the rest of their life
searching the stars?

705
00:31:35.101 --> 00:31:38.104
You don't think a person
can love a piece of art

706
00:31:38.229 --> 00:31:40.815
or music or a story so much

707
00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:42.525
that it heals them?

708
00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:45.862
Shows them parts of themselves
they'd never even seen before?

709
00:31:45.987 --> 00:31:47.155
And give them hope?

710
00:31:47.322 --> 00:31:48.489
Um...

711
00:31:49.574 --> 00:31:51.367
Well. no. of-of course I do.

712
00:31:51.492 --> 00:31:53.745
A show like this
could've gone on forever.

713
00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:55.038
Given generations

714
00:31:55.163 --> 00:31:57.498
of fans a place
to feel seen. to belong.

715
00:31:57.624 --> 00:32:00.335
something to believe in again.

716
00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:03.338
no matter who they were
or where they came from.

717
00:32:04.505 --> 00:32:08.760
But you can't do any of that
without a season two.

718
00:32:12.472 --> 00:32:15.016
Finally. a light.

719
00:32:15.141 --> 00:32:17.685
A fake fire?

720
00:32:18.436 --> 00:32:19.729
Tony had impeccable
taste in film.

721
00:32:19.854 --> 00:32:21.814
but his sense of decor
is just tragic.

722
00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:24.317
What's inside the fireplace?

723
00:32:28.529 --> 00:32:29.948
Celluloid film.

724
00:32:30.073 --> 00:32:31.950
Discontinued
in the 22nd century.

725
00:32:32.075 --> 00:32:34.160
many say to the detriment
of cinema.

726
00:32:34.285 --> 00:32:35.870
I think this is
the murder weapon.

727
00:32:40.375 --> 00:32:43.169
But it's also a recording.

728
00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:48.633
There's something on this film
the killer wanted to destroy.

729
00:32:48.758 --> 00:32:51.511
but made the same mistake
Joni did.

730
00:32:52.512 --> 00:32:54.138
Spock. we have to see
what's on this.

731
00:32:54.263 --> 00:32:56.432
All right. neutron star collapse
is imminent.

732
00:32:56.557 --> 00:32:58.351
Uhura. are scanners
receiving data?

733
00:32:58.518 --> 00:32:59.727
Right on time. sir.

734
00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:02.188
Captain. I'm detecting
a gamma burst

735
00:33:02.355 --> 00:33:04.190
heading into our flight path.

736
00:33:04.315 --> 00:33:05.858
It's heavier energy
than expected.

737
00:33:05.984 --> 00:33:07.568
The sensors are overloading.

738
00:33:09.696 --> 00:33:11.072
Shields up.

739
00:33:11.239 --> 00:33:12.156
Let's go to yellow alert.

740
00:33:12.281 --> 00:33:13.157
Ortegas. take us down.

741
00:33:13.282 --> 00:33:14.283
Steer as clear as you can.

742
00:33:14.409 --> 00:33:15.618
I'd like to.

743
00:33:15.743 --> 00:33:17.161
but we just had
a massive power drain.

744
00:33:17.286 --> 00:33:18.538
Engines aren't responding.

745
00:33:18.663 --> 00:33:20.289
Neither are shields.

746
00:33:20.415 --> 00:33:21.708
Bridge to Science Lab.

747
00:33:21.874 --> 00:33:23.543
Mr. Scott.
what's happening to our system?

748
00:33:23.668 --> 00:33:25.378
<i>SCOTTY
Well. it seems the holodeck</i>

749
00:33:25.503 --> 00:33:28.214
is pulling a wee bit more power
than I'd anticipated.

750
00:33:28.339 --> 00:33:29.841
Then shut it down.

751
00:33:29.966 --> 00:33:31.634
We have a situation on the
bridge. and I need power. now.

752
00:33:31.759 --> 00:33:32.927
<i>Yeah. it's not quite
that easy. sir.</i>

753
00:33:33.094 --> 00:33:34.220
<i>- Um...</i>
- Red alert.

754
00:33:34.387 --> 00:33:36.848
Mr. Scott.

755
00:33:36.973 --> 00:33:39.267
without engine power.
we are going to fly head-first

756
00:33:39.392 --> 00:33:40.727
into a massive gamma-ray burst.

757
00:33:40.893 --> 00:33:42.937
and we are not
going to survive it.

758
00:33:50.278 --> 00:33:51.279
Mr. Scott. I need a solution.

759
00:33:51.404 --> 00:33:53.114
Uh... I can manually

760
00:33:53.239 --> 00:33:55.658
reroute power to you
in small increments.

761
00:33:55.783 --> 00:33:57.326
<i>That should give you something.</i>

762
00:33:57.452 --> 00:33:58.745
Do it. Do it now.

763
00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:00.913
You've got roughly
30 seconds of acceleration.

764
00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:02.373
Can you make that work?

765
00:34:02.498 --> 00:34:04.500
I do like a good challenge.

766
00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:25.480
We're through.

767
00:34:27.648 --> 00:34:29.317
Let's hope
there isn't another one.

768
00:34:31.611 --> 00:34:34.739
Mr. Scott. I'd like to talk
to Lieutenant Noonien-Singh.

769
00:34:36.282 --> 00:34:39.077
<i>Uh. let me see if I can get her.</i>

770
00:34:41.245 --> 00:34:44.999
Let's say I figured out a way
to contact La'An.

771
00:34:45.124 --> 00:34:47.293
Hypothetically.

772
00:34:47.418 --> 00:34:48.961
Then you'd be
my hypothetical hero.

773
00:34:49.087 --> 00:34:50.671
I think you can't get through
in a conventional way

774
00:34:50.797 --> 00:34:52.548
because the AI is blocking out
all comms

775
00:34:52.673 --> 00:34:53.966
that don't come
from the simulation.

776
00:34:54.133 --> 00:34:55.718
It's refusing
to let her talk to anyone

777
00:34:55.843 --> 00:34:58.179
who's not part of the program.
It's-it's... it's evolving.

778
00:34:58.304 --> 00:35:01.474
So you need to convince it
you're a hologram. too.

779
00:35:03.976 --> 00:35:05.478
Oh.

780
00:35:06.771 --> 00:35:09.190
<i>I wrote the book</i>

781
00:35:09.315 --> 00:35:11.776
<i>on space jurisdiction.</i>

782
00:35:11.943 --> 00:35:14.320
<i>and I am known...</i>

783
00:35:15.363 --> 00:35:18.449
<i>...for my... diction.</i>

784
00:35:18.574 --> 00:35:19.826
<i>Now...</i>

785
00:35:19.951 --> 00:35:24.747
get me... the Agonyans.

786
00:35:26.499 --> 00:35:27.792
The Agonyans?

787
00:35:27.959 --> 00:35:29.961
-Oh... cut!
- What?

788
00:35:30.128 --> 00:35:31.462
Keep rolling. Keep rolling.

789
00:35:31.629 --> 00:35:32.630
-Okay.
- Was that too much? I-I...

790
00:35:32.755 --> 00:35:34.090
I thought it was great.

791
00:35:34.215 --> 00:35:35.216
- I completely felt it.
- Okay. okay. okay. okay.

792
00:35:35.341 --> 00:35:36.801
Okay. Quiet on the set.

793
00:35:36.968 --> 00:35:38.094
Quiet on the set.

794
00:35:38.219 --> 00:35:39.595
<i>What's the problem now?</i>

795
00:35:39.720 --> 00:35:41.973
<i>Oh. where do I even start? Uh.</i>

796
00:35:42.098 --> 00:35:44.142
maybe you could try
delivering the line

797
00:35:44.267 --> 00:35:46.352
like an actual human being
for once?

798
00:35:46.477 --> 00:35:47.979
Oh.

799
00:35:48.104 --> 00:35:50.273
Well. maybe
if you wrote a line...

800
00:35:50.398 --> 00:35:52.567
- Uh-huh. Oh.
- ...worth delivering.
I could try to.

801
00:35:52.692 --> 00:35:54.735
- Uh-huh.
- What is this scene even about?

802
00:35:54.861 --> 00:35:56.154
<i>I thought
it was about capitalism.</i>

803
00:35:56.279 --> 00:35:57.613
<i>Big surprise.</i>

804
00:35:57.738 --> 00:35:59.282
<i>You didn't understand
the script.</i>

805
00:35:59.407 --> 00:36:00.825
<i>Again.</i>

806
00:36:00.950 --> 00:36:02.827
<i>Does no one here care
about my vision?</i>

807
00:36:02.994 --> 00:36:04.162
<i>MAN
Uh. sir?</i>

808
00:36:04.287 --> 00:36:05.746
- What?!
- Uh. you...

809
00:36:05.872 --> 00:36:08.082
There's a call. Um...

810
00:36:11.377 --> 00:36:13.546
Not a good time.

811
00:36:15.423 --> 00:36:17.633
What? I...

812
00:36:19.719 --> 00:36:21.929
You can't do that. I-I...

813
00:36:22.054 --> 00:36:24.348
I am the show. You c--

814
00:36:24.473 --> 00:36:25.892
<i>I...</i>

815
00:36:26.058 --> 00:36:28.728
<i>You'll regret this.</i>

816
00:36:33.232 --> 00:36:35.067
La'An? La'An?

817
00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:38.446
<i>La'An. I don't know
if you'll see this.</i>

818
00:36:38.571 --> 00:36:40.615
<i>but it was my only chance
of getting a message to you.</i>

819
00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:42.450
<i>The holodeck is pulling
all computer</i>

820
00:36:42.575 --> 00:36:44.535
processing power
from the rest of the ship.

821
00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:47.580
The only way to stop it is
to end the program. which means

822
00:36:47.747 --> 00:36:49.498
<i>you have to solve the mystery.</i>

823
00:36:50.333 --> 00:36:52.919
Wait. If we're pulling
all processing power.

824
00:36:53.044 --> 00:36:55.880
that includes shields.
navigation. life support.

825
00:36:56.047 --> 00:36:58.299
They could die.
We could <i>all</i> die.

826
00:36:58.424 --> 00:36:59.592
An accurate assessment.

827
00:36:59.717 --> 00:37:01.302
An accurate asse...

828
00:37:01.427 --> 00:37:03.429
How are you not more concerned?

829
00:37:03.596 --> 00:37:05.223
These are our friends.
our lives.

830
00:37:05.348 --> 00:37:06.766
You asked me to remain detached.

831
00:37:06.891 --> 00:37:08.392
I am merely doing
as you requested.

832
00:37:08.517 --> 00:37:10.102
We now know that Bellows did
indeed care very much

833
00:37:10.269 --> 00:37:11.729
about the cancellation.

834
00:37:11.854 --> 00:37:14.106
Yep. That gives him motive.
and he had an opportunity.

835
00:37:14.232 --> 00:37:16.859
But he had no reason
to kill Woods <i>or</i> Lupino.

836
00:37:16.984 --> 00:37:18.611
She didn't cancel the show.
She would have done

837
00:37:18.736 --> 00:37:20.112
anything to save it.

838
00:37:20.279 --> 00:37:22.031
That's true.

839
00:37:22.156 --> 00:37:24.742
Including firing me.

840
00:37:24.909 --> 00:37:27.954
You know.
for such a smart detective.

841
00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:30.206
you're awfully stupid.

842
00:37:30.331 --> 00:37:32.625
They weren't canceling us.

843
00:37:32.792 --> 00:37:34.543
They were replacing me.

844
00:37:34.669 --> 00:37:36.003
On my own show.

845
00:37:36.128 --> 00:37:39.090
Perhaps we should sit down
and calmly discuss this.

846
00:37:39.215 --> 00:37:42.885
I came here calm. I came here
to ask for my job back.

847
00:37:43.010 --> 00:37:46.973
Do you have any idea
how humiliating that is?

848
00:37:47.098 --> 00:37:48.975
And when he said no.
that's when you--

849
00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:50.893
I didn't kill anybody!

850
00:37:51.018 --> 00:37:53.521
- Then put the gun down.
- Hmm?

851
00:37:53.646 --> 00:37:56.148
You could actually
hurt someone in here.

852
00:37:56.274 --> 00:37:57.692
Th-This isn't a game anymore.

853
00:37:57.817 --> 00:37:59.485
This was never a game.

854
00:38:01.529 --> 00:38:02.738
This is my career.

855
00:38:02.863 --> 00:38:04.782
And I'm not putting the gun down

856
00:38:04.907 --> 00:38:07.326
because I'm not the killer.

857
00:38:08.661 --> 00:38:10.955
But someone else here is.

858
00:38:11.831 --> 00:38:12.790
Me?

859
00:38:12.915 --> 00:38:14.333
- Mm.
- I was brought here

860
00:38:14.458 --> 00:38:16.252
- to solve this case.
- I know.

861
00:38:16.377 --> 00:38:19.839
Amelia Moon.
the great detective.

862
00:38:19.964 --> 00:38:23.592
Death just seems
to follow her wherever she goes.

863
00:38:23.718 --> 00:38:25.177
No. this is crazy. I--

864
00:38:25.303 --> 00:38:29.265
And your strange friend
in his odd clothes.

865
00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:31.851
so devoid of emotion.

866
00:38:31.976 --> 00:38:33.978
Always watching.

867
00:38:34.103 --> 00:38:36.355
studying.

868
00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.399
Hey. wait a minute.

869
00:38:38.524 --> 00:38:40.860
Did the network send you?

870
00:38:41.027 --> 00:38:42.611
Are you my replacement?

871
00:38:45.364 --> 00:38:46.532
No! You idiot!

872
00:38:46.657 --> 00:38:48.367
He was just here to help me!

873
00:38:48.534 --> 00:38:49.618
I-I didn't. I didn't.

874
00:38:49.744 --> 00:38:52.788
Spock? Spock?

875
00:38:52.913 --> 00:38:55.249
I'm okay.

876
00:38:55.374 --> 00:38:57.168
Oh. thank God.

877
00:39:03.966 --> 00:39:05.426
Wh...

878
00:39:16.395 --> 00:39:18.564
What is it?

879
00:39:23.194 --> 00:39:24.862
It's you.

880
00:39:25.613 --> 00:39:27.823
<i>You're</i> the killer.

881
00:39:27.948 --> 00:39:29.325
You poisoned Lupino's cup

882
00:39:29.450 --> 00:39:32.328
when you were
"running diagnostics."

883
00:39:32.453 --> 00:39:36.332
You sabotaged the chandelier.
then saved me from it.

884
00:39:36.457 --> 00:39:38.751
And then...

885
00:39:38.918 --> 00:39:40.252
you killed Woods.

886
00:39:40.419 --> 00:39:43.047
A novel theory.
but I have no motive.

887
00:39:43.214 --> 00:39:45.925
I am simply your colleague.
assisting you with a case.

888
00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:47.551
You're not even a part
of this story.

889
00:39:47.676 --> 00:39:49.261
I brought you here.

890
00:39:54.809 --> 00:39:57.436
Because you <i>made me</i>
bring you here.

891
00:39:57.561 --> 00:39:59.730
You're not my colleague.

892
00:39:59.855 --> 00:40:01.524
You're a hologram.

893
00:40:01.649 --> 00:40:05.069
You have been since the moment
I walked into the holodeck.

894
00:40:05.236 --> 00:40:08.239
I asked the computer
for a mystery made for me.

895
00:40:08.364 --> 00:40:10.825
and it gave me you.
because. well.

896
00:40:10.950 --> 00:40:14.453
it watched me earlier
with the real Spock. and...

897
00:40:16.831 --> 00:40:19.792
...it knew
I'd never suspect him.

898
00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:26.632
Bravo.
Lieutenant La'An Noonien-Singh.

899
00:40:26.757 --> 00:40:28.634
You have solved the mystery.

900
00:40:28.801 --> 00:40:32.096
Oh.

901
00:40:33.472 --> 00:40:35.474
Computer...

902
00:40:37.268 --> 00:40:38.811
...end program?

903
00:40:46.652 --> 00:40:48.696
<i>While holodeck technology</i>

904
00:40:48.821 --> 00:40:50.990
certainly holds promise.
the risks

905
00:40:51.115 --> 00:40:53.117
outweigh the potential benefits.

906
00:40:53.242 --> 00:40:55.327
We do not recommend
the device be installed

907
00:40:55.453 --> 00:40:58.164
on active ships... at this time.

908
00:40:58.289 --> 00:40:59.665
How about we recommend
that it be locked

909
00:40:59.790 --> 00:41:01.333
in a box
deep underground somewhere?

910
00:41:01.459 --> 00:41:04.170
Well. if I could just make
one wee. tiny addendum.

911
00:41:04.336 --> 00:41:05.796
It-it just needs
a dedicated server room

912
00:41:05.921 --> 00:41:07.923
- and power source that--
-Mm.

913
00:41:08.048 --> 00:41:11.010
Include it in the report.
but as a footnote.

914
00:41:11.177 --> 00:41:13.512
In small print.

915
00:41:14.763 --> 00:41:16.307
Lieutenant?

916
00:41:16.474 --> 00:41:18.934
Do you know
how many crew we have on board?

917
00:41:19.059 --> 00:41:20.436
Uh...

918
00:41:20.561 --> 00:41:22.021
- Uh...
- 203.

919
00:41:22.146 --> 00:41:26.233
Specifically. 203 of the finest
Starfleet has to offer.

920
00:41:26.358 --> 00:41:28.277
There is never any reason

921
00:41:28.402 --> 00:41:30.321
to do something alone
when you need help.

922
00:41:30.446 --> 00:41:32.198
Yeah. but what if there is?

923
00:41:33.699 --> 00:41:36.702
About a week before my last ship
ran into the Gorn.

924
00:41:36.827 --> 00:41:38.954
I was building a sensor array.

925
00:41:39.079 --> 00:41:40.581
I thought I saw some anomalies.

926
00:41:40.706 --> 00:41:42.291
but I didn't have time
to finish it.

927
00:41:42.416 --> 00:41:46.670
My CO said she would.
but she didn't. and then we...

928
00:41:49.381 --> 00:41:53.052
What happened on the <i>Stardiver</i>
wasn't your fault.

929
00:41:53.928 --> 00:41:55.846
What we do here is hard.

930
00:41:55.971 --> 00:41:57.598
and sometimes bad things happen.

931
00:41:59.141 --> 00:42:01.310
But this crew is a team.

932
00:42:02.186 --> 00:42:04.396
Knowing when to ask for help
isn't a weakness.

933
00:42:04.563 --> 00:42:06.440
It's a strength.

934
00:42:06.565 --> 00:42:08.984
One the captain and I
value very highly.

935
00:42:24.458 --> 00:42:26.669
Thank you for agreeing to finish
our lesson from earlier.

936
00:42:26.794 --> 00:42:28.796
Of course.

937
00:42:29.755 --> 00:42:31.840
Computer.
begin playlist: Spock 21.

938
00:42:33.676 --> 00:42:35.386
So I take it the holodeck
mystery test was not

939
00:42:35.511 --> 00:42:37.054
to your satisfaction.

940
00:42:39.807 --> 00:42:43.727
Well. I requested
a solo mystery.

941
00:42:43.852 --> 00:42:47.189
yet the holodeck led me
to believe I needed a partner.

942
00:42:47.314 --> 00:42:48.774
You.

943
00:42:56.448 --> 00:42:57.950
It misunderstood your prompt?

944
00:42:58.075 --> 00:43:02.037
That. I think
it understood perfectly.

945
00:43:03.205 --> 00:43:04.832
I just didn't take into account

946
00:43:04.957 --> 00:43:07.501
that the holodeck knew me
better than I realized.

947
00:43:07.626 --> 00:43:12.006
It gave me a suspect
I never anticipated.

948
00:43:12.131 --> 00:43:14.592
Interesting technology.

949
00:43:18.053 --> 00:43:20.055
How did you deduce this twist?

950
00:43:20.180 --> 00:43:21.599
I didn't at first.

951
00:43:21.724 --> 00:43:23.767
I was looking for clues
in all the wrong places.

952
00:43:23.892 --> 00:43:27.146
But now I understand the puzzle
it built for me.

953
00:43:32.526 --> 00:43:34.278
And what was that?

954
00:43:34.445 --> 00:43:36.322
It created a holographic
version of you

955
00:43:36.447 --> 00:43:38.991
that was clear-headed. logical.

956
00:43:41.118 --> 00:43:42.661
Cold.

957
00:43:54.214 --> 00:43:56.342
Words that have been used
to describe me.

958
00:43:57.217 --> 00:43:59.178
But I...

959
00:43:59.303 --> 00:44:01.472
I know you.

960
00:44:03.223 --> 00:44:05.225
I know the feel
of your arms when we dance.

961
00:44:05.351 --> 00:44:09.355
I... know the way
we look into each other's eyes.

962
00:44:09.521 --> 00:44:11.565
He didn't look at me like that.

963
00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:14.943
Or perhaps
I've misread the situation.

964
00:44:21.450 --> 00:44:24.453
Earlier today...

965
00:44:24.578 --> 00:44:27.247
I wondered if it was I
who had misunderstood.

966
00:44:27.373 --> 00:44:31.752
Because for a moment.
I thought that you wanted...

967
00:44:32.711 --> 00:44:36.256
And... did you also want?

968
00:44:52.648 --> 00:44:55.567
We will eat
the human food-pies!

969
00:44:55.693 --> 00:44:57.778
Is it... is it pies. or piles?

970
00:44:57.903 --> 00:45:00.656
-It's piles.
- Pies?

971
00:45:00.781 --> 00:45:02.157
Left.

972
00:45:02.282 --> 00:45:04.827
And back. Right. right. right.
right. right. right.

973
00:45:04.952 --> 00:45:06.412
- And right!
- Oh.

974
00:45:08.080 --> 00:45:09.832
How's this?

975
00:45:09.957 --> 00:45:11.750
How about this?

976
00:45:12.626 --> 00:45:14.795
- Goddamn it!
-Costumes!

977
00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:16.880
You guys. is it "nebula"?

978
00:45:17.047 --> 00:45:18.590
Whoa!

979
00:45:20.467 --> 00:45:22.219
- Then we'll need a leg up.
- Then we'll need a leg up.

980
00:45:22.344 --> 00:45:24.096
- That's my line!
- I thought I had the leg bit.

981
00:45:24.221 --> 00:45:25.889
- Line?
- What? Why would you
have the leg bit?

982
00:45:26.014 --> 00:45:27.975
I-I don't know.
I thought I had the leg bit.

983
00:45:28.100 --> 00:45:29.727
Well. it's clearly my line!
It's a captain's line.

984
00:45:29.852 --> 00:45:31.437
Why would she have the leg bit?

985
00:45:31.603 --> 00:45:32.730
It's my line.

986
00:45:32.855 --> 00:45:34.898
Yeah.
We're waiting on sound.

987
00:45:35.023 --> 00:45:37.234
SHAW
Red leather. yellow leather.

988
00:45:37.401 --> 00:45:39.945
Ah. ah. ah. ah.

989
00:45:40.863 --> 00:45:43.115
Hey.

990
00:45:43.282 --> 00:45:45.451
I've lost her.

991
00:45:45.617 --> 00:45:48.120
I've lost her!

992
00:45:49.204 --> 00:45:50.789
I've lost her!

993
00:45:54.585 --> 00:45:57.963
Jesus Christ. I was trying
to get my leg over.

994
00:45:58.088 --> 00:46:00.257
Can... this chair...

995
00:46:00.382 --> 00:46:03.594
Can someone fix the chair?
Please?

996
00:46:03.719 --> 00:46:05.971
I'm... I w...
I can't work like...

997
00:46:06.096 --> 00:46:07.598
Open the door.

998
00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:14.396
Help. Help!

999
00:46:14.521 --> 00:46:16.148
- Whoa. whoa. there!
- Yeah. I got you.

1000
00:46:16.273 --> 00:46:19.026
Captioning sponsored by
CBS

1001
00:46:19.151 --> 00:46:22.988
Captioned by
Media Access Group at WGBH
access.wgbh.org
